# {{ project_name }}: working agreements

Instructions for coding agents and humans alike. `CLAUDE.md` imports this file.

## Commands

Run everything through `just` (installed in the project environment, so
`uv run just <recipe>` always works).

| Recipe | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `just setup` | Install dependencies and git hooks |
| `just check` | Everything CI runs. Run before declaring work done |
| `just fmt` | Auto-format and auto-fix |
| `just lint` | ruff, pyproject-fmt{% if is_django %}, djlint{% endif %} |
| `just typecheck` | ty |
| `just arch` | Import Linter contracts and the test mirror check |
| `just test [pytest args]` | Tests with coverage (80% branch coverage floor) |
{%- if is_django %}
| `just up` / `just migrate` / `just dev` | Postgres, migrations, Django + Vite dev servers |
| `just frontend-check` | Biome, `tsc`, production Vite build |
{%- endif %}

## Architecture

The package is split into layers. A layer may import the layers below it and
never the layers above. Import Linter enforces this (`just arch`); the
contracts live in `[tool.importlinter]` in `pyproject.toml`.

```
src/{{ package_name }}/
{%- if is_django %}
  interface/     entry points, split into api/ (JSON) and ui/ (HTML, htmx, admin)
{%- elif is_cli %}
  interface/     entry points: the command line
{%- endif %}
  usecases/      orchestrate one task end to end; the only layer that calls vendors
  domain/        business rules and strategies
  vendors/       clients for external systems, one package per system
  data/          {% if is_django %}Django models, queries and data structures{% else %}persistence and data structures{% endif %}
  utils/         generic helpers with no business knowledge
```

Allowed imports, top to bottom:
`{% if not is_library %}interface > {% endif %}usecases > domain | vendors > data > utils`.

Rules that follow from this:

- **Organise by feature inside each layer**: `domain/<feature>/`,
  `data/<feature>/`{% if is_django %}, `interface/ui/<feature>/`, `interface/api/<feature>/`{% endif %}.
  `usecases/` is flat, one module per feature.
- **Interface stays thin.** Parse input, call a use case or a domain rule,
  present the result. No business logic in {% if is_django %}views, templates or admin{% elif is_cli %}CLI commands{% else %}the public API module{% endif %}.
- **Only usecases call vendors.** Domain rules never reach an external system:
  a use case fetches from the vendor and hands plain values to the domain.
  {%- if not is_library %} Interface never imports vendors directly.{% endif %}
- **Vendors are standalone.** They import `utils` and nothing else of ours, so
  they return their own types, not our models.
- **Domain and vendors do not import each other.**
- **A new top-level package must be assigned a layer** in the `layers`
  contract, otherwise `just arch` fails (`exhaustive = true`).
- **Do not weaken a contract to make a change pass.** If an import is
  forbidden, the code is in the wrong layer; move it.
{%- if is_django %}
- **One Django app.** The package itself is the app, so `migrations/`,
  `templates/`, `static/` and `management/` sit at the package root. Models
  live in `data/<feature>/models.py` and must be imported in `models.py`,
  which is how Django finds them. Admin classes live in `interface/ui/admin.py`.
- **Settings** are declared once, typed, in `config.py`, and read elsewhere via
  `django.conf.settings`. Nothing outside `settings/` imports `config.py`.
- **htmx.** Views return a full page, or a template partial
  (`render(request, "feature/page.html#partial")`) when `request.htmx` is set.
  htmx is vendored at `static/vendor/`; update it with `just vendor-htmx`.
- **Frontend.** TypeScript and {% if is_tailwind %}Tailwind{% else %}Bootstrap{% endif %} live in `frontend/src` and are built by
  Vite. Prefer htmx attributes over custom TypeScript.
{%- if is_tailwind %} Style with Tailwind utility classes in the templates;
  Tailwind only emits classes it finds in `src/` and `frontend/src`, so never
  build class names by string concatenation.
{%- else %} Style with Bootstrap's components and utility classes;
  theme it by overriding its CSS variables in `frontend/src/main.css`.
{%- endif %}
{%- elif is_cli %}
- **Configuration** is loaded by the interface layer from `config.py` and
  passed down as plain values. Lower layers never read the environment.
{%- else %}
- **Public API.** Only names re-exported from `{{ package_name }}/__init__.py`
  are public.
{%- endif %}

## Tests

```
tests/
  unit/          no I/O{% if is_django %}, no database{% endif %}; mirrors src/{{ package_name }}/
  integration/   {% if is_django %}database, views, tasks{% else %}real I/O and entry points{% endif %}; mirrors src/{{ package_name }}/
  functional/    user-visible flows end to end; free-form layout
{%- if is_django %}
  factories/     factory_boy factories
{%- endif %}
```

- `tests/unit/domain/x/test_y.py` must correspond to `src/{{ package_name }}/domain/x/y.py`.
  `just arch` checks this; exceptions go in `[tool.test-mirror]`.
- Every test function has a one-line docstring stating the behaviour.
- Warnings are errors in tests. Fix the cause instead of filtering.

## Conventions

- Python 3.14, fully type annotated, checked by ty.
- ruff is the linter and formatter; its rule set in `pyproject.toml` is the
  style guide. Prefer fixing code over adding `noqa`; every `noqa` names its rule.
- Public modules, classes and functions have docstrings.
- Dependencies are managed with uv (`uv add`, `uv add --dev`). Commit `uv.lock`.
- Commit messages follow Conventional Commits (`feat:`, `fix:`, ...); release
  notes are generated from them.
- This project was generated from a Copier template. Shared tooling changes
  belong in the template and arrive via `copier update`.
